\" alt=\"Illustration of the Statue of Liberty in headphones with musical notes in the background\"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Joy du jour </strong>from reader Claire Duchene of Brooklyn, N.Y.:</p><ul><li><strong>\"Lately, I've taken up </strong>cycling in Prospect Park. The other day I was on a ride, and a man was blasting music from his boombox and dancing for the amusement of all passing cyclists and runners. At first, with my headphones on, I did what most New Yorkers would do and ignored the man.\"</li><li><strong>\"On my second pass,</strong> I paid more attention and couldn't help but smile at his infectious enthusiasm, dance moves and cheering of the passing runners, a few of whom danced and jammed along with him as they passed. ... That stranger made my day with his expression of pure joy and desire to share it with the world.\"</li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/48263930.425679/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtOGFlZGYzZTYtMjEyNy00MWYzLWE5OTItY2JkNmRhNzQ5MGZmLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QyJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkyJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aC4be9bb19\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/i